  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Brown recluse family—Loxoscelesspp. (11 species indigenous to United States); 8–15 mm in body size; legs 2–3 cm long; violinshaped pattern on cephalothorax with the neck of the fiddle extending caudally; 6 eyes (3 paired diads) as opposed to other spider species with 8 eyes; active at night.",
              "pdf_page": 1357,
              "printed_page": 1275,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Distribution—found throughout the Midwest from the Gulf Coast of the United States and up the Mississippi River valley to southern Iowa. Some species are found in eastern southern California, western Arizona, and southern New Mexico.",
              "pdf_page": 1357,
              "printed_page": 1275,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "60% of “brown recluse” spider bites in humans are misdiagnosed as they occur in areas with no endemic spider populations. See extensive Differential Diagnosis.",
              "pdf_page": 1357,
              "printed_page": 1275,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Bites usually occur when spider becomes trapped in bedding; induce, necrotic arachnidism, an indolent dermatonecrotic lesion mediated by the venom enzyme sphingomyelinase D, direct hemolysis of erythrocytes, platelet aggregation, renal failure, coagulopathy, and death.",
              "pdf_page": 1357,
              "printed_page": 1275,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Currently no consensus on treatment and multiple therapies have been used, including antihistamines, corticosteroids, dapsone, hyperbaric oxygen, surgical excision, and antivenom.",
              "pdf_page": 1357,
              "printed_page": 1275,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Antivenom currently unavailable in United States (available only in Argentina, Brazil, and Mexico).",
              "pdf_page": 1357,
              "printed_page": 1275,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
